---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import CodeBlock from 'web-design-system/astro/product/CodeBlock.astro'
import TableScroll from '../../components/TableScroll.astro'
import { REPO_URL } from '../../data/docs'
import { FORMATS, RELEASES_URL } from '../../../data/downloads'

const toc = [
  { label: 'Download a bundle', href: '#download' },
  { label: 'macOS', href: '#macos' },
  { label: 'Linux', href: '#linux' },
  { label: 'Windows', href: '#windows' },
  { label: 'First launch', href: '#first-launch' },
  { label: 'From source', href: '#from-source' },
]

const requirements = [
  { need: 'macOS', detail: 'macOS 12 or newer. The bundle sets that as its minimum system version.' },
  { need: 'Linux', detail: 'The Rust crates build on Linux; GPUI needs the platform graphics and input libraries.' },
  { need: 'Windows', detail: 'Windows 10 or newer. The GPUI backend compiles its shaders with the Windows SDK.' },
  { need: 'Rust', detail: 'A toolchain new enough for edition 2024 (1.85+), plus the platform GUI dependencies.' },
  { need: 'Nix (optional)', detail: 'The dev shell provides the pinned toolchain, cargo-watch, Node, and pnpm — see Development setup.' },
]

const appimage = `# Make it executable and run it; there is no install step.
chmod +x todo-lofi-<version>-linux-x86_64.AppImage
./todo-lofi-<version>-linux-x86_64.AppImage`

const debian = `# Installs into /usr; the package carries its own libraries.
sudo apt install ./todo-lofi-<version>-linux-x86_64.deb
todo-lofi`

const tarball = `tar -xzf todo-lofi-<version>-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
./todo-lofi/usr/bin/todo-lofi`

const clone = `git clone ${REPO_URL}.git
cd todo-lofi

# Run the desktop app
cargo run -p todo-2`
---

<Docs
  title="Install and run"
  description="Download a prebuilt bundle for macOS, Linux, or Windows, or build todo-lofi from source."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="warning" title="Pre-release">
    The bundles are development builds of the current version and are not signed or notarized. macOS
    needs a one-time confirmation on first launch — see <a href="#macos">macOS</a> — and Linux and
    Windows run them as they are.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="download">Download a bundle</h2>
  <p>
    Every release carries one bundle per platform, named after its version. The newest is{' '}
    <span data-download-version>the latest release</span>:{' '}
    <a href={RELEASES_URL}>all downloads and versions</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/downloads">downloads page</a> picks the right file for the machine you are on.
    From here, pick your platform below — each link points at the newest matching file.
  </p>

  <h3 id="macos">macOS</h3>
  <ul>
    {
      FORMATS.macos.map((format) => (
        <li>
          <a data-download data-platform="macos" data-format={format.id} href={RELEASES_URL}>
            <code data-download-name>{format.label}</code>
          </a>
          {' — '}
          {format.hint}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <ol>
    <li>Download the <code>.dmg</code> and open it, or download the <code>.zip</code> and unzip it.</li>
    <li>Drag <code>todo-lofi.app</code> into your <code>/Applications</code> folder.</li>
    <li>
      Open the app once. Gatekeeper stops an unsigned build the first time: go to
      <strong>System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security</strong>, scroll to the security notice, and
      choose <strong>Open Anyway</strong>. Right-clicking the app in the Finder and choosing
      <strong>Open</strong> does the same thing.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3 id="linux">Linux</h3>
  <ul>
    {
      FORMATS.linux.map((format) => (
        <li>
          <a data-download data-platform="linux" data-format={format.id} href={RELEASES_URL}>
            <code data-download-name>{format.label}</code>
          </a>
          {' — '}
          {format.hint}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    The AppImage runs without installing anything. The other two fit the usual layouts: the{' '}
    <code>.deb</code> installs under <code>/usr</code>, and the tarball unpacks anywhere.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={appimage} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={debian} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={tarball} showLineNumbers={false} />

  <h3 id="windows">Windows</h3>
  <ul>
    {
      FORMATS.windows.map((format) => (
        <li>
          <a data-download data-platform="windows" data-format={format.id} href={RELEASES_URL}>
            <code data-download-name>{format.label}</code>
          </a>
          {' — '}
          {format.hint}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <ol>
    <li>Download the <code>.zip</code> and extract it.</li>
    <li>
      Run <code>todo-lofi.exe</code>. SmartScreen may warn about an unsigned build the first time:
      choose <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="first-launch">First launch</h2>
  <p>
    The app starts with an empty database. Nothing leaves your machine: there is no account to create
    and no telemetry, and the network features stay off until you configure them.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="/docs/tasks">Tasks, tags, and projects</a> — the four pages that follow cover the app itself, from the task list to the agent pane.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/configuration">Configuration</a> — the in-app settings panes, plus model providers, Todoist, and GitHub credentials.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A task list works offline out of the box; sync and the agent are the parts that need credentials.
  </p>

  <h2 id="from-source">From source</h2>
  <p>
    Everything below builds todo-lofi from a checkout instead of downloading a bundle. Use it if you
    are working on the code, if your platform has no bundle, or if you would rather not run a
    prebuilt binary.
  </p>

  <h3 id="what-you-need">What you need</h3>
  <TableScroll label="Requirements">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>You need</th>
          <th>Details</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          requirements.map((requirement) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{requirement.need}</td>
              <td>{requirement.detail}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    The least fiddly path is the development shell, because it brings the toolchain and every helper
    command with it. <a href="/docs/contributor/development">Development setup</a> walks through installing Nix and direnv;
    if you already have a workable Rust toolchain, you can skip it.
  </p>

  <h3 id="from-a-checkout">Run it from a checkout</h3>
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={clone} showLineNumbers={false} />

  <h3 id="macos-app">Build a macOS app</h3>
  <p>
    On macOS, the dev shell's <code>t2-bundle</code> assembles a real <code>todo-lofi.app</code> for you —
    it builds the binary, copies the icon and <code>Info.plist</code> into the bundle, writes the
    Cargo.toml version into the bundle's <code>Info.plist</code>, signs it with the
    <code>todo-lofi-dev</code> identity when that certificate exists, and clears the quarantine flag. It
    assembles the bundle without launching it.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is also how release builds are produced, so it is the closest thing to a local install. For
    everyday development, run the raw binary instead (<code>t2</code>, or{' '}
    <code>cargo run -p todo-2</code>) — see{' '}
    <a href="/docs/contributor/development#command-reference">the command reference</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>todo-2 --version</code> prints the version the build was made from, and the same number is
    shown in the app's <strong>Settings → About</strong> pane.
  </p>
</Docs>

<script>
  import { initDownloads } from '../../../data/downloads'
  void initDownloads()
</script>
